<abstract id="abstract">
<p id="p-0001" num="0000">A query cache for a distributed ledger index tracks the append position of each shard and invalidates cached result pages only when a newer block touches a key range referenced by the page. Cache entries are scored by recomputation cost so that expensive range scans are retained longer than point lookups.</p>
</abstract>
<description id="description">
<heading id="h-0001" level="1">BACKGROUND</heading>
<p id="p-0002" num="0001">Append-only ledgers are frequently queried by range.</p>
</description>
<us-claim-statement>What is claimed is:</us-claim-statement>
<claims id="claims">
<claim id="CLM-00001" num="00001">
<claim-text>1. A method comprising:
<claim-text>receiving a range query directed to a ledger index partitioned into shards;</claim-text>
<claim-text>determining, for each shard, an append position at which a cached result page was computed; and</claim-text>
<claim-text>returning the cached result page when no block appended after the append position modifies a key in the range.</claim-text>
</claim-text>
</claim>
<claim id="CLM-00002" num="00002">
<claim-text>2. The method of <claim-ref idref="CLM-00001">claim 1</claim-ref>, further comprising scoring each cached result page by an estimated recomputation cost.</claim-text>
</claim>
<claim id="CLM-00003" num="00003">
<claim-text>3. The method of <claim-ref idref="CLM-00002">claim 2</claim-ref>, wherein pages having a higher score are evicted last.</claim-text>
</claim>
</claims>
</us-patent-grant>
